    "cost_of_believing_attribution_share": 0.3421487086703477,
    "statement": "On a budget of BRL 2,125,000, allocating from last-click attribution leaves BRL 1,692,325 (34.2%) of incremental revenue on the table against an allocation built from the true response curves. Computed inside the simulation; a statement about the methods, not about any real market."
  },
  "method_notes": {
    "linear_versus_curved": "An experiment and an attribution report each return one number per channel and say nothing about what the next dirham earns, so a planner holding only those must treat returns as constant. Only the media-mix model offers curvature, and the difference between its curved allocation and its own linear one isolates what that curvature is worth.",
    "why_not_average": "Reconciliation here is not averaging. Averaging a biased estimator with an unbiased one gives a biased estimator with a smaller variance, which is worse to hand a decision-maker than either input because it looks more trustworthy than it is."
  },
  "provenance": {
    "caveat": "SYNTHETIC — the channel-spend panel and every media effect in it are simulated from a pre-registered data-generating process, not measured. The revenue baseline is real Olist history; the media contribution layered on it is not. No figure here describes the effectiveness of any real marketing channel, and none may be quoted as one. What is measured is whether a method recovers a truth that is known only because it was constructed.",
